# DESCRIPTION

There are the three standard streams, `stdin`, `stdout` and `stderr`, with file descriptors 0, 1 and 2, and the file streams `fopen` and `fdopen` make over a descriptor.

## The standard streams

The library keeps no buffer for output to the standard streams: writes to `stdout` and `stderr` go straight to the runtime, which gathers `stdout` by line.
A process started with standard output and error rings writes to them.
Any other writes `stdout` as the process's debuglog, one record for each line; a line of more than 216 bytes is split into records of that size, and a partial line stays in the library until its newline or until `fflush`, a write to `stderr`, a read of `stdin`, or `exit`.
`stderr` is its own ring when the process has one for descriptor 2, and `stdout`'s output flushed at once otherwise.
Writing to `stdin` fails with `EBADF`.

`stdin` is read through a buffer of 4096 bytes from the process's standard input ring, or is empty if the process has none; standard output is flushed before each read.
`stdout` and `stderr` can't be read: reading them fails and sets the error indicator and `errno` to `EBADF`.
The standard streams don't seek (`ESPIPE`), and `setvbuf` on one is accepted and changes nothing.

## File streams

`fopen` opens <path> with open(2) (fcntl(2)) and makes a stream over it.
<mode> is C's: `r` reads, `w` writes a file it creates or truncates, `a` writes at the end of a file it creates if needed; `+` after it both reads and writes; `b` changes nothing, `x` adds `O_EXCL` and `e` `O_CLOEXEC`.
Another mode fails with `EINVAL`; otherwise `fopen` fails as `open` does, and a program built without the file library (fdio) finds it failing with `ENOENT`.
`fdopen` makes a stream over the descriptor <fd>, which it doesn't check.
`fclose` writes out what is pending, closes the descriptor and frees the stream.

A file stream is fully buffered, through 16 KiB of its own, unless `setvbuf` gives it another <mode> or a <buffer> of <size> bytes of the caller's; `setbuf` is `setvbuf` with `_IOFBF` and `BUFSIZ`, or `_IONBF` for a null <buffer>.
A line-buffered stream is written out after each write with a newline in it, an unbuffered one at each write.
Reading reads ahead a buffer at a time, unbuffered or not, and reads of more than the buffer go straight to the caller's memory.
A stream may switch between reading and writing without an `fflush` or `fseek` between, which C requires: the switch writes out what is pending, or gives back what was read ahead.
`fflush` of a file stream writes out what is pending and, for one read ahead, moves the descriptor back to the stream's position; `fflush(NULL)` does that for every open file stream, as `exit` does.

`fseek` and `fseeko` move the stream to <offset> from the start, the current position or the end (`SEEK_SET`, `SEEK_CUR`, `SEEK_END`), after writing out what is pending, and clear the end-of-file indicator; `ftell` and `ftello` return the position, counting what is buffered.
`rewind` seeks to the start and clears the error indicator, and `fgetpos` and `fsetpos` keep a position in an `fpos_t`.
`ungetc` pushes <c> back to be read next and clears the end-of-file indicator: a byte always, and more while the bytes before it are still in the buffer; on `stdin` likewise, into its buffer.

## Output

`printf`, `fprintf`, `vprintf` and `vfprintf` write the formatted text to a stream (`printf` to `stdout`) and return the number of bytes written.
`snprintf` and `vsnprintf` write at most <n> bytes including the terminating NUL, and return the length the whole output has, as C99 does; with <n> of 0 they write nothing and <s> may be null.
`sprintf` and `vsprintf` are `snprintf` and `vsnprintf` without a limit.
`asprintf` and `vasprintf` put the text in a buffer of malloc's (stdlib(2)), which the caller frees, store it in <s> and return its length, or -1 if there is no memory.
The `v` forms take a `va_list` that they copy, so the caller's is left as it was.

`fputc`, `putc` and `putchar` write one byte and return it as an `unsigned char`, or `EOF` on error.
`fputs` and `puts` write a string (`puts` adds a newline, and neither writes the terminating NUL) and return 0, or `EOF` on error.
`fwrite` writes <size> times <n> bytes and returns the number of whole items written.

## Formats

A conversion is `%`, then flags, a width, a precision and a length, then the conversion character.
The flags are `-`, `+`, space, `#` and `0`.
The width and the precision are numbers or `*`; a negative `*` width is `-` with its magnitude, and a negative `*` precision is none.
The lengths are `hh`, `h`, `l`, `ll`, `j`, `z`, `t` and, for floating point, `L`.
The conversions are:

: `d i`
  A signed decimal integer.
: `u o x X`
  An unsigned decimal, octal or hexadecimal integer.
: `f F e E g G a A`
  A `double`, or with `L` a `long double`: x87's 80-bit format on amd64, binary128 on arm64 and rv64.
  The digits are exact: the result is the correctly rounded decimal expansion of the value, whatever its size.
: `c`
  A byte.
: `s`
  A string; a null pointer prints `(null)`, as glibc does.
: `p`
  A pointer, as `%#lx`; a null pointer prints `(nil)`.
: `%`
  A percent sign.

An unknown conversion ends the formatting and the function returns -1; the text before it has been written.
`%n`, `%b`, the wide-character conversions `%lc` and `%ls` (read as `%c` and `%s`), `%m` and positional arguments (`%1$d`) are not supported.

## Input from a string

`sscanf` and `vsscanf` read <s> as C23's `scanf` reads its input, and return the number of items assigned, or `EOF` if the input ended before the first conversion.
White space in <format> skips any white space, another character must match itself, and `%%` matches `%`.
A conversion is `%`, an optional `*` (read, but assign nothing), a width, a length (`hh`, `h`, `l`, `ll`, `j`, `z`, `t`, `L`), and one of `d i o u x X p` (an integer as stdlib(2)'s strtol family reads it, `i` by its prefix; `p` a pointer, in hexadecimal), `a e f g` and their capitals (a `float`, with `l` a `double`, with `L` a `long double`, as strtod reads it: decimal, hexadecimal, `inf`, `infinity`, `nan`), `s` (bytes up to white space, then a NUL), `c` (the width's bytes, 1 without one, no NUL), `[` (a scanset: bytes in it, or with `^` not in it; `]` first is in it, and `a-z` is a range) and `n` (the bytes read so far, not an item).
All but `c`, `[` and `n` skip white space first.
A number is the longest start of the input, within the width, that is or begins the conversion's form; one that isn't a whole number is a matching failure.

## Reading

`fgetc`, `getc` and `getchar` return the next byte as an `unsigned char`, or `EOF` at the end of input or on an error.
`fgets` reads up to <n> - 1 bytes, stopping after a newline, terminates them with a NUL and returns <s>; it returns a null pointer if it read nothing.
`fread` reads up to <n> items of <size> bytes and returns the number of whole items read.

`feof` and `ferror` return the end-of-file and error indicators of a stream; `clearerr` clears both.
On `stdin` the end-of-file indicator is not sticky: the next read asks the ring again.

## Other calls

`fileno` returns the stream's descriptor.
`flockfile` and `funlockfile` do nothing, and `ftrylockfile` returns 0 (the lock is taken).
`remove` removes a file or an empty directory, and `rename` renames a file; they are the calls of unistd(2) and stat(2) under C's names, and fail the same way.
A program built without the file library finds both failing with `ENOENT`.

