# DIAGNOSTICS

`fclose` of a standard stream flushes it and returns 0; the stream stays open.
`fclose`, `fflush` and the writes return `EOF` (or a short count) and set the error indicator when the descriptor's write fails.

# BUGS

`fprintf` and `vfprintf` on `stderr` write through `stdout`'s path, not `stderr`'s: the text appears with standard output, buffered to the end of the line, and does not go to the stderr ring.
`fputs`, `fwrite` and `fputc` on `stderr` do go to it.
A program that needs its diagnostics on standard error uses `fwrite` or `write` of unistd(2).

A file stream has no lock: two threads may not use one at once.
There is no `scanf` or `fscanf` from a stream, no `freopen`, `tmpfile`, `popen`, `perror` or `getline`, and no wide-character streams.
Mixing `getchar` with `read(0, ...)` loses data: the stream keeps what it read ahead.
`fread` and `fgets` on `stdin` cannot tell an error from the end of input.
Switching a stream over a pipe from reading to writing loses what it had read ahead.
